[36m(PPOTrainer pid=3432520)[0m INFO 03-03 22:22:10 ppo_trainer.py:441] [trace] step=125 reward=0.000 response_len=884 total_len=2654 preview='systemYou are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.\\nKnowledge cutoff: 2024-06\\nCurrent date: 2026-03-03\\n\\nReasoning: medium\\n\\n# Valid channels: analysis, commentary, final. Channel must be included for every message.\\nCalls to these tools must go to the commentary channel: \'functions\'.developer\\n\\n# Tools\\n\\n## functions\\n\\nnamespace functions {\\n\\n// Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_exact_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).\\ntype get_heavy_atom_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).\\ntype get_mol_logp = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).\\ntype get_tpsa = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.\\ntype get_hbd = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.\\ntype get_hba = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return rotatable bonds count.\\ntype get_num_rotatable_bonds = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.\\ntype get_fraction_csp3 = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).\\ntype get_mol_mr = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return total ring count.\\ntype get_ring_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic ring count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_rings = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).\\ntype get_formal_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.\\ntype get_qed = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heteroatom count (non C/H).\\ntype get_num_heteroatoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.\\ntype describe_high_level_fg_fragments = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string to parse.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.\\ntype remove_salts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.\\ntype predict_pka = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.\\ntype estimate_logd = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Target pH (default: 7.4).\\nph?: number\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Alert library to screen against (default: all).\\nalert_library?: "all" | "pains" | "pains_a" | "pains_b" | "pains_c" | "brenk" | "nih" | "zinc" | "chembl" | "chembl_bms" | "chembl_lint" | "chembl_mlsmr"\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return fraction of aromatic atoms.\\ntype get_fraction_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic atom count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_max_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_min_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_max_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_min_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Bertz complexity index.\\ntype get_bertz_ct = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Balaban J topological index.\\ntype get_balaban_j = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Mutagenicity means the ability of a drug to induce genetic alterations. Drugs that can cause damage to the DNA can result in cell death or other severe adverse effects. Nowadays, the most widely used assay for testing the mutagenicity of compounds is the Ames experiment which was invented by a professor named Ames. The Ames test is a short-term bacterial reverse mutation assay detecting a large number of compounds which can induce genetic damage and frameshift mutations.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not mutagenic (B) is mutagenic\\nDrug SMILES: CC(=O)c1cc(N)ccc1OCC(O)CNC(C)(C)CPlease think step by step and use tools when helpful. Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe need to predict mutagenicity of a drug from SMILES. We can approximate by checking structural alerts: presence of known mutagenic substructures: e.g., anilides, nitro groups, aromatic amines, etc. The SMILES: CC(=O)c1cc(N)ccc1OCC(O)CNC(C)(C)C. Let\'s parse: "CC(=O)" acetyl group attached to aromatic ring: "c1cc(N)ccc1" - an aniline (amine) ring with amine (NH2). Then "OCC(O)CNC(C)(C)C" substituent: O-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2- N - C(C)(C)C (tert-butyl). So we have an aniline (primary amine) attached to aromatic ring. Primary aromatic amines are potential mutagens (especially if substituted). Also presence of anilide? Actually not an amide; it\'s an aniline. The presence of anilines is known mutagenic. Also the molecule has a tertiary butyl group; no obvious toxicophores. But the presence of an aniline likely triggers mutagenic alerts. The Ames test often flags anilines. So likely mutagenic. But could be not? Let\'s consider: The structure: 4-(2-hydroxy-3-(tert-butylamino)propoxy)-2-aminophenyl acetate? Actually the acetyl group at position 1? The ring has an amine at position 2? The ring has an acetyl group at position 1? Hard to parse. But still an aniline.\\n\\nThus likely B. We can also run structural alerts using tool. Use score_structural_alerts.assistantcommentary to=functions.score_structural_alerts json{"smiles":"CC(=O)c1cc(N)ccc1OCC(O)CNC(C)(C)C","alert_library":"all"}functions.score_structural_alerts to=assistantcommentary{"result": "{\\n  \\"library\\": \\"all\\",\\n  \\"count\\": 6,\\n  \\"alerts\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"aniline\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"aniline\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"analine\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_SureChEMBL\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_SureChEMBL\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Ketone\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_MLSMR\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_MLSMR\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Long aliphatic chain\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_MLSMR\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_MLSMR\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"aniline\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_LINT\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_LINT\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}", "function_name": "score_structural_alerts", "arguments": {"smiles": "CC(=O)c1cc(N)ccc1OCC(O)CNC(C)(C)C", "alert_library": "all"}}assistantanalysisThe alerts include aniline, which is a mutagenic structural motif. So likely mutagenic. Therefore answer B.assistantfinalAnswer: (B) is mutagenic'
